# ===========================================================================
# Network/API abuse protection — github.com/oarkflow/tcpguard
#
# This is a separate policy language from bcl/*.bcl (tcpguard's own BCL
# dialect: pack/guard/detector/rule/threat_model, not ref's
# resource/intent/route), loaded by the "guard" resource in
# bcl/01_resources.bcl (`resource "guard" { kind "security.tcpguard" config
# { path "./security" } }`). ref/platform's request pipeline
# (platform/routes.go's serve) evaluates every request through it before
# authentication runs — declaring the resource is the only wiring needed.
#
# What's shipped here is deliberately one rule, not tcpguard's full example
# pack (account takeover, payment velocity, admin-export-after-hours, ...):
# those need either data this starter doesn't have (a real threat-intel
# feed, a payments/admin surface) or an *outcome* the guard was never told
# about — see the note on auth-abuse-velocity below. Shipping a rule that
# looks real but can never fire is worse than not shipping it; add the
# richer example pack from tcpguard's own repo once this app actually needs
# it, and wire the event reporting note below when you do.
#
# The rule itself lives in rules/application-attack-probe.bcl, a nested
# subdirectory next to this file — LoadTCPGuardBundleDir (bundle_parse.go)
# walks "path" recursively and merges every ".bcl" file it finds, so a
# pack/guard/detector declaration and its rules can be split across as many
# files and directories as a real policy needs, the way tcpguard's own
# example packs (one file per detector/rule/threat_model) do.
# ===========================================================================

pack "starter" {
  version "1.0.0"
  mode enforce
}

guard "starter" {
  mode enforce
  version "1.0.0"
}

policy_safety {
  max_detector_timeout 25ms
  max_action_timeout 2s
  max_actions_per_rule 4
}

# Tuned down from tcpguard's own defaults (10MB) for a starter's traffic —
# this only affects the "abuse" detector's derived signals below, not any
# hard body-size limit; that's route.max_body_bytes (see 04_routes.bcl) or
# per-route rate_limit, both handled by ref/platform itself, not by this.
detector "abuse" {
  type abuse
  window 10m
  scan_path_threshold 6
}

# NOT shipped, and why: tcpguard's own example pack includes an
# "auth-abuse-velocity" rule (brute-force login protection, keyed on
# abuse.auth.ip_failures / .user_failures). Those counters only increment
# when something tells the guard a login attempt *failed* — this platform's
# guard integration (platform/resources_security.go) evaluates every
# request generically as `request.received`; it does not yet know that
# POST /login came back 401. Wiring that requires either the auth.login
# action itself reporting the outcome, or the guard middleware inspecting
# the response after ctx.Next() and reporting a synthetic
# auth.login_failed/auth.login_success event — neither exists yet. Add it
# before adding this rule back, or it will sit in this file doing nothing.
